      {
        "number": 9,
        "internalPageId": 9,
        "title": "1. Most of the verbs take “-s” when using the third person rule. take – takes cook - cooks 2. Verbs that finish in “-sh”, “-ch”, “-s”, “-ss”, “-z”, “-x”, “-o”, receive “-es” when using the third person rule. 3. Verbs that finish in “-y” change to “-i” + “es” when using the third person rule.* play – plays* stay – stays*",
        "visibleTexts": [
          "1. Most of the verbs take “-s” when using the third person rule. take – takes cook - cooks 2. Verbs that finish in “-sh”, “-ch”, “-s”, “-ss”, “-z”, “-x”, “-o”, receive “-es” when using the third person rule. 3. Verbs that finish in “-y” change to “-i” + “es” when using the third person rule.* play – plays* stay – stays*",
          "work – works stop – stops",
          "wash – washes teach – teaches",
          "study – studies copy – copies",
          "*When the “y” is preceded by a vowel letter, just add “s” at the end of the verb.",
          "HINT When talking about Jobs and referring to an individual, we need to use the articles a – an (also called indefinite). i.e. “John is an architect.”, “Peter is a doctor.” As you can see in the examples, ‘a’ is used before words that start with consonants and “an” before words that start with vowels.",
          "fix – fixes go - goes"
        ],
